{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Who has counted the dust of Jacob.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): Who can count the children of Jacob, who are like dust, and who can give a number to the quarter-part of Israel? The expression 'the quarter of Israel' refers to one banner, for there were four banners. <br>And he said, <b>Let my soul die the death of the upright</b> — for he desired to die, provided only that his end be like the end of Israel, who are the portion of HaShem and not the portion of the stars and constellations like himself; and he saw his end through this wisdom, that he was destined to fall by the sword, and therefore he prayed concerning this." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'Who has counted the dust of Jacob' — who has counted the commandments that Israel fulfill with dust: (Deuteronomy 22:10) ״לא תחרוש בשור ובחמור״, (Leviticus 19:19) ״שדך לא תזרע כלאים״, the ashes of the red heifer, the dust of the sotah — end. And these commandments are few in number, but the intent is concerning the reward of these commandments, which has no end to their goodly reward. Another interpretation: Who has counted the dust of Israel — those who have attained to the sins of a woman yet conquer their inclination and do not transgress." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel), it is possible to interpret 'Who has counted the dust of Jacob' as an allusion to the matter of the resurrection of the dead. For after he explained the time of redemption at the end of days, when Israel shall dwell alone, he immediately appended the matter of the resurrection of the dead, and said: 'Who has counted the dust of Jacob' — these are they who sleep in the dust, for at the time of resurrection they have no count and number; and so it is written (Daniel 12:2): ״ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו״. He used two expressions with them — count and number — because Israel are likened to stars, and it is written (Psalms 147:4): ״מונה מספר לכוכבים״. And he used two expressions with them — Jacob and Israel — based on what has already been explained, that Jacob alludes to the body and Israel alludes to the soul; and so the prophet says (Isaiah 43:1): ״בוראך יעקב ויוצרך ישראל״ — he ascribed creation (בריאה) to Jacob and formation (יצירה) to Israel; and so it is written (ibid. 45): ״יוצר אור ובורא חשך״ — he ascribed creation to darkness and formation to light. Observe that he said 'the dust of Jacob' and did not say 'the dust of Israel,' and said 'the quarter of Israel,' to allude that the soul shall return to the four elements of the dust. And he said, <b>Let my soul die</b> — the word 'my soul' in this place refers to the body, for the soul does not die; and so (Leviticus 5:1): ״ונפש כי תחטא״, (ibid. [5:]7) ״והנפש האוכלת״ — for the rational soul neither sins nor eats, and we testify that it is the body that eats; but because the body is nothing without the soul, and the soul is the essence, therefore Scripture calls the body by the expression 'soul.' And he revealed to us here the matter of the world of souls and the matter of the world to come, which is after the resurrection — which is the ultimate goal and the hope of every hope, and it is the final good that is hidden and stored away. And it is well known that the world of souls is what comes to a person immediately, straightaway after separation from this world; therefore he asked to merit, after death, immediately the world of souls — this is the death of the upright; and he asked further to merit, at the end of time, the world to come, which is prepared for whoever is now in the world of souls; and this is what he said: 'Let my end be like his' — for the world to come, which is after the resurrection, is the end of the world of souls and the culmination of all pleasures. And we learn from the entirety of this discourse, which recounts the praise of Israel and their great excellence from the standpoint of their beginning and their end — that they shall inherit all of this world, that they shall merit the resurrection of the dead for the life of the world to come that follows the resurrection — and he explained in this that Israel alone inherit all of this, to the point that he himself, when he came to curse, blessed himself with their blessing. And had we heard such wondrous promises from the mouth of one prophet of Israel's prophets, our glory and splendor would not be so great as now, when we have heard them from the mouth of a prophet of the nations of the world, who is our adversary and is more reliable in this than any other — for he is an evil messenger and answers Amen against his will."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:53.873711540Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}